From e5770a95443558d0e6f91093c46915d10cc1be81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:12:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 18/30] windows: expose the guest-IPC vsock configuration
 surface

With the device built on Windows, the configuration path that reaches it can
stop being Unix-only. `vmm_config::vsock`, `VsockConfig`, `VsockBuilder`,
`VmResources::vsock` and `attach_unixsock_vsock_device()` all lose their gates;
`krun_add_vsock_port()`, `krun_add_vsock_port2()` and the `unix_ipc_port_map`
they feed lose the -ENOTSUP stubs added in 1f9791b.

What stays Unix-only is TSI. `krun_set_port_map()` still refuses, so
`VsockDeviceConfig::host_port_map` is always `None` on Windows, and the
`tsi_hijack`/`tsi_hijack_unix` cmdline tokens are still only appended there --
handing them to a guest with no TSI backend would strand it.

`krun_add_vsock()` becomes a single implementation instead of a Windows stub and
a Unix version. Enabling the device with no feature bits is precisely what a
guest-IPC-only consumer asks for and now works everywhere, so only a non-empty
`TsiFlags` is refused with -ENOTSUP. Leaving the whole call refused would have
made the work in the preceding commits unreachable: `krun_add_vsock_port2()`
returns -ENODEV unless the device has been enabled first.

Verified: `cargo build --target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc -p libkrun --release`
links, producing a PE32+ x64 krun.dll with the same 59 exports as before this
series' vsock work and a new ws2_32.dll import table. `cargo check -p libkrun`
is at 0 errors for both x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu and aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.
Nothing has been run on Windows.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
